<h1>JVM Container Memory — Theory &amp; Layout</h1>
<div class="subtitle">Personal reference. All values approximate; units MiB unless noted. Bar widths are to scale.</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="part-heading">Part 1 — Theory</div>

<h2>The setup</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Confirmed config: <code>-Xms7000m -Xmx7000m</code>, container limit <strong>8Gi (8,192 MiB)</strong>, Java 8 with <strong>Parallel GC</strong> (not G1 — confirmed via JVM startup flags, no <code>-XX:+UseG1GC</code> present).</li>
  <li><code>-Xms = -Xmx</code> means the JVM starts with the full 7,000 MiB heap committed from the JVM's perspective, but Linux RSS only grows as those committed pages are actually touched and backed by physical memory.</li>
  <li>The JVM commits the heap, but physical pages usually become RSS only after they are touched. That is why a 7,000 MiB committed heap does not necessarily appear as 7,000 MiB RSS at process start.</li>
  <li>Live heap usage observed peaking at <strong>~6.6 GiB</strong> — close to the 7000m ceiling, leaving only ~420 MiB heap margin at peak.</li>
</ul>
<div class="analogy">
Analogy: it's like renting a 7,000 MiB warehouse and signing a lease that says you'll <em>never downsize</em>.
You pay for the whole space from day one — whether you're storing 1 box or 6,000.
</div>

<h2>Why containers don't start at 7,000 MiB <span class="confidence high">High confidence</span></h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>-Xms = -Xmx</code> commits the full 7,000 MiB from the JVM's perspective on day one — but Linux RSS reflects only resident pages, not the full commit.</li>
  <li>Committing ≠ touching physical pages. Linux uses demand paging: physical memory (RSS) is only consumed when a page is actually written to for the first time.</li>
  <li>The JVM touches some heap during initialization and early allocation, but not necessarily the whole 7,000 MiB — unless <code>-XX:+AlwaysPreTouch</code> is enabled. So containers realistically start around <strong>3–4 GiB RSS</strong>.</li>
  <li>As the app runs, live objects spread into previously-untouched regions, those pages get faulted in, and RSS climbs.</li>
  <li>With <strong>Parallel GC</strong> on Java 8 and <code>-Xms = -Xmx</code>, we should not rely on meaningful heap uncommit/shrink behavior. Once heap pages are touched and resident, RSS is expected to stay high and trend toward the Xmx ceiling.</li>
</ul>
<div class="analogy">
Analogy: signing the 7,000 MiB warehouse lease on day one doesn't mean movers fill every shelf immediately —
it means no one else can rent that space. You start by moving boxes into 3–4 GiB worth of shelves.
As more inventory arrives, you occupy more of the leased space — but you never give shelves back once stacked,
and you never need a new lease, because you already signed for the whole 7,000 MiB.
</div>

<h2>Why this matters for RSS / cgroups <span class="confidence high">High confidence</span></h2>
<ul>
  <li>RSS (Resident Set Size) is what the cgroup memory limit actually counts against.</li>
  <li>Once heap pages are touched, they count toward RSS — <strong>even if the objects on them are garbage</strong> waiting for GC.</li>
  <li>With Parallel GC and <code>-Xms=-Xmx</code>, we should not rely on meaningful heap uncommit/shrink behavior — once touched, heap pages typically stay resident.</li>
</ul>
<div class="analogy">
Analogy: the warehouse doesn't care whether the boxes inside are full of useful inventory or empty cardboard —
the landlord (cgroup) only cares that you're occupying the whole 7,000 MiB footprint.
</div>

<h2>Heap floor vs. non-heap: why total RSS isn't perfectly flat <span class="confidence high">High confidence</span></h2>
<p>The pinned-ceiling model applies specifically to <strong>heap memory</strong>. Non-heap regions behave differently — each component has its own reclaim story:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Metaspace</strong> — not in GC's remit directly. Reclaim is only triggered when an entire classloader becomes unreachable and a GC cycle detects it. For a typical application classloader, classes loaded at startup stay loaded permanently — metaspace is effectively static for the container's lifetime.</li>
  <li><strong>JIT code cache</strong> — not GC's remit at all. Managed entirely by the JIT compiler, which independently discards deoptimized or aged-out compiled methods. GC has no involvement.</li>
  <li><strong>Thread stacks</strong> — not GC's remit at all. OS-managed; allocated on thread creation, freed on thread exit. For a long-lived thread pool (Tomcat, Spring), stacks are allocated once and stay — only pool shrinkage releases them.</li>
  <li><strong>Direct buffers</strong> — eligible but indirect. The GC collects the Java <code>DirectByteBuffer</code> wrapper object on the heap, which triggers a <code>Cleaner</code> callback that frees the native memory. GC is involved, but one step removed — and only fires when heap GC pressure is high enough to collect the wrapper.</li>
</ul>
<p>So occasional small, non-drastic dips in total reported RSS are expected — they reflect direct buffer reclaim and thread pool fluctuation, not the heap floor moving. Only direct buffers have meaningful GC involvement, and even that is indirect.</p>
<div class="analogy">
Analogy: the warehouse (heap) lease is locked at 7,000 MiB and shelves never get removed once stacked.
But the small office, loading dock, and break room (non-heap) have month-to-month furniture —
desks get added or removed as staffing changes. Total square footage in use can dip slightly when
furniture is removed, even though the warehouse itself never shrinks.
</div>

<h2>Where the OOMKill comes from <span class="confidence high">High confidence</span></h2>
<p>The cgroup limit covers <em>all</em> memory the process uses — not just heap:</p>
<ul>
  <li>committed heap (up to Xmx = 7,000 MiB)</li>
  <li>+ non-heap memory (~440 MiB observed: metaspace, compressed class space, code cache, etc.)</li>
  <li>+ thread stacks / JIT code / native libs (~300 MiB combined)</li>
  <li>+ direct buffers</li>
</ul>
<p>Under the current 8Gi (8,192 MiB) limit: 8,192 − 7,000 − 440 − 300 ≈ <strong>~450 MiB headroom</strong>. With live heap peaking at ~6.6 GiB, there's very little slack before the cgroup OOM killer fires on the <em>total</em>. Numbers are approximate — JVM flags use MiB-style units while Kubernetes limits are expressed as Gi/MiB; the conclusion is unaffected.</p>
<div class="analogy">
Analogy: the warehouse lease is 7,000 MiB, but you also need a small office, a loading dock, and a break room
(metaspace, threads, native libs). If the warehouse takes up almost the entire 8,192 MiB lot,
there's barely room left for the office — eviction notice.
</div>

<h2>On OS reclaim of freed heap memory <span class="confidence high">High confidence</span></h2>
<p>Lowering Xms gives the JVM a floor to shrink toward after a large post-GC occupancy drop — but whether this results in the OS reclaiming pages is load-pattern dependent, not guaranteed:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>QA (controlled conditions):</strong> with artificially high load and a sharp 30-minute session-expiry cliff, a large synchronized release of garbage caused a post-GC occupancy drop dramatic enough to trigger Parallel GC's shrink policy — RSS dropped from 6.1 GiB to 3.9 GiB. This demonstrated that the mechanism <em>can</em> work.</li>
  <li><strong>UAT (real-world load patterns):</strong> did not reproduce. Sessions expire at different times, load is more continuous — post-GC occupancy never dropped far enough below <code>MaxHeapFreeRatio</code> in a single cycle to trigger meaningful uncommit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Conclusion: OS reclaim of freed heap is <strong>not a reliable mechanism</strong> under real-world load. Lowering Xms is still worth doing for the startup/low-load RSS benefit, but should not be framed as a solution to OOMKill risk. The only reliable fix is the container limit increase.</p>

<h2>The recommendation <span class="confidence high">High confidence</span></h2>
<p>Because live heap peaks at ~6.6 GiB, <strong>Xmx cannot be lowered below that</strong> — doing so would force premature full GCs or risk heap-OOME before the retention fixes land.</p>
<p><strong>Primary recommendation:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Container limit: 8Gi → <strong>10Gi (10,240 MiB)</strong></li>
  <li><code>-Xms3g -Xmx7500m</code></li>
  <li>Result: ~920 MiB heap margin above the observed peak, and ~2,000 MiB non-heap/native/container cushion (vs. ~450 MiB today).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Fallback if 10Gi is blocked:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Keep container at 8Gi, keep <code>Xmx=7000m</code></li>
  <li>Lower <code>Xms</code> to 3g only</li>
  <li>Improves startup/low-load RSS trajectory, but does <strong>not</strong> change the ~420 MiB margin at peak load — containers remain at elevated OOMKill risk until the retention fixes deploy.</li>
</ul>
<div class="analogy">
Analogy: instead of shrinking the warehouse lease (which doesn't fit your inventory), you negotiate a bigger lot (10Gi)
and keep enough warehouse space (7500m) to comfortably hold your ~6.6 GiB of actual inventory, with real room left over
for the office, dock, and break room. If the landlord won't give you the bigger lot, at least stop moving boxes into
every shelf on day one (lower Xms) — but the lot is still almost full once business picks up.
</div>

<div class="verdict">
<strong>Bottom line:</strong> the mechanism is consistent with known JVM/container memory behavior — committed/touched heap
contributes to RSS, RSS counts against the cgroup limit, and Java heap occupancy alone does not determine OOMKill risk.
Xms/Xmx tuning here is a <strong>headroom/capacity fix</strong>, independent of the application-level
memory retention issues already confirmed via heap dumps and documented separately.
</div>

